## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for init, malloc, and free
typedef struct Node { void* next; } Node;
static void* head;

void init_arena(void* arena_base, size_t size) {
  // split arena into 32-byte blocks and link on free-list
}

void* my_malloc() {
  void* old = atomic_load(&head);
  void* next = ((Node*)old)->next;
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, &old, next)) return old;
  // retry loop omitted for brevity
}

void my_free(void* p) {
  Node* node = (Node*)p;
  void* old;
  do {
    old = atomic_load(&head);
    node->next = old;
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, &old, node));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Low-level memory management on constrained hardware.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size allocator (slab)
- Free-list stored inside blocks; index-based chaining
- O(1) allocations/deallocations with minimal metadata
- Alignment, memory overhead, and fragmentation considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct {
  uint32_t next; // index of next free slot
} FreeSlot;

#define POOL_SIZE 256
#define SLOT_SIZE 64
static uint8_t pool[POOL_SIZE * SLOT_SIZE];
static uint32_t head = 0; // index of first free slot
static uint32_t used = 0;

void slab_init(void) {
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < POOL_SIZE - 1; ++i) {
    ((uint32_t*)pool)[i] = i + 1;
  }
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[POOL_SIZE - 1] = UINT32_MAX;
  head = 0;
  used = 0;
}

void* slab_alloc(void) {
  if (head == UINT32_MAX) return NULL;
  uint32_t idx = head;
  head = ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx];
  ++used;
  return pool + idx * SLOT_SIZE;
}

void slab_free(void* p) {
  ptrdiff_t idx = ((uint8_t*)p - pool) / SLOT_SIZE;
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx] = head;
  head = (uint32_t)idx;
  --used;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect fragmentation in this design?
- How would you extend this to multi-threaded contexts with minimal locking?

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces a simple but non-trivial producer-consumer synchronization at the memory-ordering level, with attention to cache effects and correctness under contention.

## Key Concepts
- Bounded circular buffer
- Lightweight per-slot synchronization flags
- Memory ordering: release/acquire
- False sharing and cache-line padding

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-C-like snippets
let head = 0, tail = 0, N = 1<<12;
function enqueue(slot, msg){
  const idx = head & (N-1);
  // write payload, then set READY
  slots[idx].payload = msg;
  Atomics.store(slots[idx].flag, 1); // READY
  head++;
}
function dequeue(){
  const idx = tail & (N-1);
  if (Atomics.load(slots[idx].flag) === 1){
    const msg = slots[idx].payload;
    Atomics.store(slots[idx].flag, 0); // EMPTY
    tail++;
    return msg;
  }
  return null;
}
``` 

